<description>
**Problem:** Kernel applications can only override the time validation uses for the whole process, with `btck_set_mock_time` (#35496). Every chainstate manager in the process sees the same time, so a time set for one test, run, or thread leaks into the others unless the application remembers to reset it.

**Solution:** Add `btck_chainstate_manager_options_set_clock_time` and `btck_chainstate_manager_set_clock_time`, which set the time for a single chainstate manager, before it is created (so it also applies to checks made while it loads the chainstate) and afterwards. Validation code gets the time from a new `ChainstateManager::Now()` method instead of calling `NodeClock::now()` directly. Details are in the commit messages.

Keeping the time in the chainstate manager instead of in a global makes kernel applications more reliable, because state from one run or thread can't affect another.

Other commits:

- The first commit fixes a rare timing race in the `chainstatemanager_ibd_exit_after_loading_blocks` test, where test code and validation code read the clock separately and assumed they got the same time.
- The last two commits switch that test and the `utxo_total_supply` fuzz target to the new per-chainman clock, as suggested in https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35557#issuecomment-4763429282.

Two related commits that were previously part of this PR are in a follow-up branch based on it, https://github.com/ryanofsky/bitcoin/commits/pr/kclock2: a mempool refactor to represent clock times consistently, and a change that makes `NodeClock::now()` unavailable in libbitcoinkernel so kernel code can't use the system clock by accident.
</description>

<commits>
79c304eacd test: Fix nondeterministic clock race in chainstatemanager_ibd_exit_after_loading_blocks

The test set up the tip time using Now<NodeSeconds>() and then called
UpdateIBDStatus(), which calls IsTipRecent(), which also calls
Now<NodeSeconds>(). If a second boundary was crossed between the two
calls, the tip_recent=true case would fail spuriously.

Fix by freezing the clock with FakeNodeClock before the test runs.

Co-Authored-By: seduless <seduless@proton.me>
This change was written with Claude Sonnet 4.6.

98bb3d3114 kernel, validation: scope clock time to ChainstateManager

Add ChainstateManager::Now() as the single injection point for the
current time in all validation paths, replacing direct NodeClock::now()
calls. A new m_clock_now_seconds field (std::atomic<NodeSeconds>)
lets callers override the time for a chainstate manager instance.

Affected paths:
- ContextualCheckBlockHeader: future-time check
- UpdateIBDStatus -> IsTipRecent: IBD latch (IsTipRecent gains a now
  parameter so callers control what time is used)
- ProcessNewBlockHeaders / ReportHeadersPresync: IBD progress logging
- GuessVerificationProgress: verification_progress in block tip callbacks
- VerifyLoadedChainstate: future-tip sanity check on startup

The kernel C API gains btck_chainstate_manager_set_clock_time(chainman,
now_seconds), and the C++ wrapper gains a matching SetClockTime() method.
The time can also be set before the chainstate manager is created, with
btck_chainstate_manager_options_set_clock_time(), so it also applies to
checks made while the chainstate is loaded (the VerifyLoadedChainstate
future-tip check and the IBD latch), which run before
btck_chainstate_manager_set_clock_time() can be called.
Two concurrent test instances using different chainman objects see
independent clocks; no cleanup of global state is required between tests.

Note: this commit slightly changes behavior of the
IsCurrentForFeeEstimation and GuessVerificationProgress functions
because these function previously did time calculations with 1-second
precision, and use with system clock level precision (typicaly
nanoseconds). No other behavior outside of these two functions is
changing.

Co-Authored-By: stringintech <stringintech@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: MarcoFalke <*~=`'#}+{/-|&$^_@721217.xyz>
This change was written with Claude Sonnet 4.6.

a4c62cdd8c test: Use chainman-scoped clock in chainstatemanager_ibd_exit_after_loading_blocks

Replace SetMockTime() with chainman.m_clock_now_seconds so the clock
frozen for the race-prevention matches the same clock that
UpdateIBDStatus/IsTipRecent() reads. This also avoids touching any
global state and lets the test more directly exercise the new
chainman-scoped clock path.

This change was written with Claude Sonnet 4.6.

2f6b902f98 test: Use chainman-scoped clock in utxo_total_supply fuzz target

Replace FakeNodeClock (global mock time) with chainman.m_clock_now_seconds
so the fuzzed time is set directly on the chainstate manager instance
rather than via a global. This exercises the IBD and tip-age checks
through the new per-chainman clock path. Byte consumption order from
fuzzed_data_provider is unchanged so existing corpus entries remain valid.

Idea for using clock time in utxo fuzz test from sedited in:
https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35557#issuecomment-4763429282

This change was written with Claude Sonnet 4.6.
</commits>

